  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 5.3. Increased capacity of Member States to design and deliver innovative, flexible and inclusive learning options, encompassing work-based learning and quality apprenticeships</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">In October 2023, the Government through the Ministry of Employment in consultation with social partners and stakeholders across the skills ecosystem developed the Seychelles Skills Mobility Framework to ensure effective skills mobility governance and efficient labour migration management, to enhance employability, labour market responsiveness and entrepreneurship performance through improved skills anticipation (recognition of present skill needs and rapid labour shortages&#x2019; projections), skills transfer programmes and skills recognition mechanisms. The framework was based on ILO standards, methodologies and tools related to Recognition of Prior Learning (RPL) and the mutual recognition of qualifications. The Ministry also undertook a rapid skills needs assessment in circular economy in Seychelles which intends to identify and measure skills mismatches and anticipate future skills needs. There were some other actions on skills targeting youth, mainly the development of hybrid training modules on decent employment and entrepreneurship in the circular economy allowing to train 20 young people in youth entrepreneurship in the blue economy on the basis of the two training modules finalised in English and French. With support from the SDG Funds, the ILO has conducted a skills need assessment on how to promote decent work and sustainable entrepreneurship in the field of circular economy. The ILO also developed a report of the related results by using its methodology and according to its &#x2018;Guidelines on rapid assessment of reskilling and upskilling needs in response to the COVID-19 crisis&#x2019;. The ILO also conveyed several consultation meetings and one virtual tripartite validation workshop on 14 June 2023 to discuss the draft report and undertook a technical revision of the final version. The report was validated based on the ILO and the stakeholders&#x2019; comments. The results of this analysis were used to produce hybrid (in room and online) training modules on the promotion of decent work and sustainable entrepreneurship in the field of circular economy in collaboration with ITC Turin. 

With support from the European Union, the ILO supported the country to develop the Seychelles&#x2019; Skills Mobility Framework. The ILO also conveyed several consultation meetings and one virtual tripartite validation workshop from January to March 2023 to discuss the draft framework and undertook a technical revision of the final version. The framework was validated based on the ILO and the stakeholders comments.</narrative>
   </description>
